<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tāwhiao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tūkāroto Matutaera Pōtatau Te Wherowhero Tāwhiao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tāwhiao was the second Māori King, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potatau-te-wherowhero">Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</a> in 1860. During the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> in 1863–1864 his people lost their lands, and he withdrew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-country">King Country</a>, where he led his followers for nearly twenty years. He made peace with the government in 1881, travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1884 to seek redress from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> without success, and founded the Kauhanganui council.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tawhiao.jpg" alt="Tāwhiao" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gottfried Lindauer (1839-1926) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tawhiao_Matutaera_Potatau_Te_Wherowhero,_by_Gottfried_Landauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
